INSERT INTO `wp_options` (`option_id`, `option_name`, `option_value`, `autoload`) VALUES ('327', '_transient_wpmedia_mcp_oauth_wellknown_health_check', 'a:6:{s:5:\"label\";s:29:\"MCP OAuth discovery documents\";s:6:\"status\";s:11:\"recommended\";s:5:\"badge\";a:2:{s:5:\"label\";s:13:\"Configuration\";s:5:\"color\";s:4:\"blue\";}s:11:\"description\";s:515:\"<p>The following .well-known discovery document(s) returned a bare 404 with no WordPress-originated response header: oauth-protected-resource, oauth-authorization-server. This matches the fingerprint of a physical .well-known/acme-challenge/ directory (provisioned by the host for Let\'s Encrypt auto-SSL) intercepting the request before WordPress runs — likely the cause here, though this should be confirmed against the actual .well-known/acme-challenge/ directory on the server before concluding root cause.</p>\";s:7:\"actions\";s:740:\"<p>Apply the Apache or Nginx snippet from this library\'s README (\"Hosting: .well-known conflicts\") to re-enable routing for these two paths without touching acme-challenge/.</p><p>If a CDN or page cache is in front of the site, purge it after applying the fix — a stale cached 404 for these paths can otherwise persist even once the server config is corrected.</p><p>This check runs from the server to itself. A \"Good\" result here does not guarantee external clients can reach these documents: a CDN, WAF, or reverse proxy in front of the site can still 404 these paths for external traffic while the server\'s own loopback request bypasses it. Verify with an external <code>curl</code> request after applying any server-config change.</p>\";s:4:\"test\";s:37:\"wpmedia_mcp_oauth_wellknown_discovery\";}', 'off');
